      "text": "To find the minimum possible value of the expression \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\\), we will apply the Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality (AM-GM Inequality), which states that the arithmetic mean of non-negative real numbers is greater than or equal to their geometric mean.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the given expression for clarity:\n\\[\nS = \\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\n\\]\n\nWe will apply the AM-GM Inequality to the three terms:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} \\geq 3 \\sqrt[3]{\\left(\\frac{a}{2b}\\right) \\left(\\frac{b}{4c}\\right) \\left(\\frac{c}{8a}\\right)}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to compute the product inside the cube root:\n\n\\[\n\\left(\\frac{a}{2b}\\right) \\left(\\frac{b}{4c}\\right) \\left(\\frac{c}{8a}\\right) = \\frac{a \\cdot b \\cdot c}{2b \\cdot 4c \\cdot 8a} = \\frac{abc}{64abc} = \\frac{1}{64}\n\\]\n\nNow, we take the cube root of this product:\n\n\\[\n3 \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{64}} = 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{4} = \\frac{3}{4}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum value of the original expression is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{4}\n\\]\n\nTo verify that this minimum value is indeed achievable, we need to check if there exist positive real numbers \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) such that the equality condition of AM-GM Inequality holds. The equality in AM-GM Inequality holds if and only if all the terms are equal. Hence, we set:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} = \\frac{b}{4c} = \\frac{c}{8a}\n\\]\n\nLet \\( k = \\frac{a}{2b} = \\frac{b}{4c} = \\frac{c}{8a} \\). Then:\n\n\\[\na = 2bk, \\quad b = 4ck, \\quad c = 8ak\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( b = \\frac{a}{2k} \\) and \\( c = \\frac{b}{4k} \\) into \\( c = 8ak \\):\n\n\\[\nc = 8a \\left(\\frac{a}{2k}\\right) = \\frac{8a^2}{2k} = \\frac{4a^2}{k}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( b = 4ck \\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2k} = 4k \\cdot \\frac{4a^2}{k}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying, we get:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{2k} = 16a \\implies a = \\frac{1}{32k^2}\n\\]\n\nSimilarly, substituting back to find \\( b \\) and \\( c \\):\n\n\\[\nb = \\frac{a}{2k} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{32k^2}}{2k} = \\frac{1}{64k^3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nc = \\frac{b}{4k} = \\frac{\\frac{1}{64k^3}}{4k} = \\frac{1}{256k^4}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting these into the original expression yields equality, confirming that the minimum value is indeed \\(\\frac{3}{4}\\).\n\nThus, the minimum possible value of the sum is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4}}\n\\]",
