      "text": "To find the minimum value of the sum \\(\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a}\\) where \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) are positive real numbers, we can apply the AM-GM inequality (Arithmetic Mean - Geometric Mean inequality) in a strategic way.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} + \\frac{b}{4c} + \\frac{c}{8a} \\geq 3 \\left(\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{a}{2b} \\cdot \\frac{b}{4c} \\cdot \\frac{c}{8a}} \\right).\n\\]\n\nTo simplify the expression inside the cube root, notice how the terms simplify together. Let's first determine the possible manipulation:\n\nOur goal is to find \\(\\left(\\frac{a}{2b} \\cdot \\frac{b}{4c} \\cdot \\frac{c}{8a}\\right).\\) Using the rules of algebra for fractions:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} \\cdot \\frac{b}{4c} \\cdot \\frac{c}{8a} = \\frac{a \\cdot b \\cdot c}{2b \\cdot 4c \\cdot 8a}.\n\\]\n\nSimplified from this, find the terms that can be combined:\n\nA simplified manipulation:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{a}{2b} \\cdot \\frac{b}{4c} \\cdot \\frac{c}{8a} = \\frac{abc}{2 \\cdot 4 \\cdot 8abc}.\n\\]\n\n\\(\\hline a, b, c\\) cancel out giving us:\n\n\\[ \\frac{1}{64} \\]\n\nTaking the cube root of the result:\n\n\\[ \\sqrt[3]{\\frac{1}{64}} = \\frac{1}{4}. \\]\n\nSo through order of operations:\n\nThe next stage follows:\n\nHence finally:\n\n\\[\n3 \\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) = \\frac{3}{4}. \\]\n\nTherefore,\n\n\\boxed{\\frac{3}{4} % }",
